and new warning signs that a recession  could be on the way  all three major stock indexes took a  serious beating to start the week and  we've got our vlad duterte here  following the story vlad i used to work  on wall street you know this area what  are we looking at here we're looking at  tony a bear market which generally means  an index like the dao has fallen 20  percent or more from its high the s p  fell into bear territory yesterday  closing at more than 20 percent below  its january high the dow is down more  than seven percent from just a week ago  dropping 876 points just yesterday now  all eyes are now on the federal reserve  and it's chairman jerome powell who's in  a tough spot here's why if the fed  raises interest rates later this week  experts say it could send the market  into deeper bear territory if the fed  does not raise rates inflation could  keep rising and right now gas is at more  than five dollars a gallon food prices  are up 10 in the last year and the cost  of travel is up 38  and all those things could go even  higher vlad thank you so much so with  the cost of living going up and our net  worth going down let's bring in cbs news  business analyst jill schlesinger with  some perspective and hopefully some help  for those watching so how significant is  a bear market and what does it mean for  the everyday american well look i mean  it's significant they don't happen that  often but about a dozen times since  world war ii it depends on how long bear  markets last and to really determine  that we got to wait for the future and  that's so hard to do so if you look at  our most recent bear market in 2020 it  was really short we had a five-week  period where the s p 500 lost a third of  its value then it came roaring back  sometimes a bear market can be a  self-fulfilling prophecy for a recession  it's not inevitable but it can mean that  there's a leading indicator it means  that corporations aren't making as much  money investors are kind of wigged out  and consumers are really freaked out  also we had a university of michigan  confidence index released friday it was  at a 50-year low so consumers feel less  confident today than they did amid the  worst part of covent i think this is  significant didn't jamie dimon just say  the other day get ready for an economic  hurricane is this what he was talking  about yeah i think that this is not  exactly what he was talking about  because a lot of ceos whether it's  whether it's jamie dimon or james gorman  or david solomon the three big  investment banks what they're talking  about is the economy more than the stock  market the stock market can recover  pretty quickly the economy we're not so  sure about right now we're at a  crossroads as vlad said we have a  federal reserve meeting it's starting  today and the federal reserve has to  thread this needle they want to slow  down the pace of inflation but by  raising interest rates right what's  happening is they could actually tip us  into a recession if they go too far too  fast and the the real hurricane could be  if we're in a recession  still having high prices that's called  stag a stagnating economy with high  prices stagflation is someone out there  with a job offer on the table now does  that mean you should take it rather than  wait out well listen the one part of the  economy that has been strong is  consumers continue to spend you know if  you look at the airport travel data we  are seeing people on airplanes despite  those 38 increases in airfares so people  are spending jobs are actually plentiful  right now so yes i think it is a good  time to take that job or solidify it but  be clear when we have recessions it is  important to go back to your financial  basics that emergency reserve fund  paying down your debt and if you are  still working don't stop putting money  in the market this is your time to force  yourself to buy at lower levels right  the aggressive good advice thank you  jill thank you
 

